===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of one who [[proceed]]s, or who prosecutes a design or transaction
# {{lb|en|especially in plural}} An [[event]] or [[happening]]; something that [[happen]]s
#* '''1919''', Rita Wellman, ''The Wings of Desire''
#*:He had often painted himself at a mirror, a tortuous and fascinating '''proceeding''', as every artist knows, and had been forced to admire the way in which he was made.
#* '''1836''', {{w|Charles Dickens}}, ''{{w|The Pickwick Papers}}'' Chapter 50
#*:It was with feelings of no small astonishment, when the carriage drew up before the door with the red lamp, and the very legible inscription of ‘Sawyer, late Nockemorf,’ that Mr. Pickwick saw, on popping his head out of the coach window, the boy in the gray livery very busily employed in putting up the shutters—the which, being an unusual and an unbusinesslike '''proceeding''' at that hour of the morning, at once suggested to his mind two inferences: the one, that some good friend and patient of Mr. Bob Sawyer’s was dead; the other, that Mr. Bob Sawyer himself was bankrupt.
# The collection of academic papers published in the context of an academic conference
# Progress or movement from one thing to another.
#* {{quote-book|year=1898|author={{w|Winston Churchill (novelist)|Winston Churchill}}
|title={{w|The Celebrity}}|chapter=2
|passage=I had occasion […] to make a somewhat long business trip to Chicago, and on my return […] I found Farrar awaiting me in the railway station. He smiled his wonted fraction by way of greeting, […], and finally leading me to his buggy, turned and drove out of town. I was completely mystified at such an unusual '''proceeding'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|measure taken in business, transaction}} A measure or step taken in a course of business; a transaction
#: {{ux|en|an illegal '''proceeding'''}}
#: {{ux|en|a cautious or a violent '''proceeding'''}}
#* {{w|Macaulay}}
#*: The '''proceedings''' of the high commission.
# {{lb|en|legal}} Any legal action, especially one that is not a [[lawsuit]].
#* '''December 7 2016''', Kelly Phillips Erb writing in {{w|Forbes}}, ''[http://www.forbes.com/sites/kellyphillipserb/2016/12/07/house-says-no-to-renewed-efforts-to-impeach-irs-commissioner/#38083e3d942d House Says No To Renewed Efforts To Impeach IRS Commissioner]''
#*:Since impeachment is a legal '''proceeding''', while anyone can make a motion to start the process, the Judiciary Committee determines whether there are sufficient grounds for impeachment.

